# Write so a tired reader does not have to decode

When you create or edit Markdown, emails, recaps, tickets, or product docs in this repo, every sentence must carry a full idea. The reader should not need an earlier chat, a Convex file, or a status-machine in their head.

## Do this

- Use ordinary punctuation and full sentences. Lists are fine. A list item is still a complete thought, not a pile of nouns.
- Name the who, the what, and the outcome. Weeks later, “apply” and “the cut” mean nothing.
- If you use a status or a role, say what a person does at that step.
- After you write, re-read as someone who has not seen the rest of the pack. If a line only makes sense because you already know the design, rewrite it.

## Do not do this

- Shorthand because the idea was already said once in this chat or another file.
- Noun catalogs after a label (`Buildings, units, tenants, open work orders…`).
- Slogan cuts (`The login can stay. Access does not.` / `Not X. Y.`).
- Engineer compression (`Work order status moves received, then assigned…`).
- Colon dumps that are not a sentence.

## Example

Bad: Work order status moves received, then assigned, then scheduled, then done.

Good: A repair starts as received, then staff assign it, then it is scheduled, then it is marked done. Those steps happen in that order.

Bad: Scale. Small internal. Ten buildings, three owners.

Good: This is a small internal tool. About ten buildings, three owners, office staff, some caretakers, and the tenants of those buildings.
